{
  "result": "There is a kind of light that only exists at 6:12 a.m. on a Tuesday. It's not the light of sunrise photos or the golden hour they chase for weddings. It slips through the blinds and makes the dust in your kitchen look like a small galaxy, and for a minute you remember you are living inside an ongoing life, not just preparing for one.\n\nWe talk about starting things \u2014 new habits, new cities, new versions of ourselves. We love the narrative of the breakthrough, the overhaul, the reinvention. But almost no one writes poems about maintenance. About doing the dishes again. About watering the same plant for the third year in a row. About returning to the same running path even though no one is clapping for you.\n\nYet maintenance is where most of life actually happens. Love, for example, is not sustained by a single gesture but by a thousand invisible repairs. Remembering how they take their coffee. Apologizing quickly. Staying when it would be easier to drift.\n\nI think we've been tricked into believing that if something is not extraordinary, we are failing. But maybe the extraordinary thing is that it continues at all. That you kept the friendship alive across time zones. That you kept showing up to the page even when the writing was bad.\n\nThere is dignity in upkeep. In sweeping the floor knowing it will get dirty again. It is a rebellion against entropy, a vote for the world you want to keep.",
